|---|---|---|
| Frontend UI, components, pages, forms, tables, dialogs, a11y, responsive | `saas-frontend-ux.md` (as adapted in Assessment §3), AMSDP UI/UX assessment, shared components (`frontend/components/`), `frontend/lib/i18n.js` | none installed → governance docs |
| Backend API / service endpoints | `saas-application-security.md` (adapted), Business Rules Parts II–X | none installed → governance docs |
| Authentication, login, sessions, RBAC, permissions | `saas-auth-and-authorisation.md` (adapted), Business Rules §7–8, `backend/common/permissions.py`, M2 security report | none installed → governance docs |
| Multi-tenant data, org/branch isolation | `saas-multitenant-data.md` (adapted), Business Rules §4–6, `backend/common/auth.py` scope checks | none installed → governance docs |
| Database schema, migrations, indexes, data integrity | `saas-multitenant-data.md` §4–5, `saas-cicd-workflow.md` §3 (adapted), Alembic chain, `backend/common/models.py` | none installed → governance docs |
| Dashboard / analytics / metrics | `saas-frontend-ux.md` + `saas-observability.md` (adapted), dashboard aggregate endpoints, Business Rules §8 | none installed → governance docs |
| Queue / scheduling / workflow transitions / tokens | Business Rules Parts V–VII, `saas-api-throttling.md` (concurrency, adapted), M4/M5 implementation reports | none installed → governance docs |
| Audit / data integrity / reporting | `saas-observability.md` (adapted, redaction + denial recording), `backend/common/audit.py`, Business Rules Part X | none installed → governance docs |
| Docker, deployment, ports, infrastructure | `saas-cicd-workflow.md` (adapted, git safety + honest CI state), `docker-compose.yml`, M6 constraints | none installed → governance docs |
| LLM / AI tooling, agent execution routing | §8 LLM Execution Routing Policy, AILI architecture docs | none installed → governance docs |
| Documentation: reading project docs, writing reports, doc placement or migration | §12 (reading) and §11 (writing), `docs/governance/AMSDP_Docs_Index_V1.0.md`, `docs/governance/AMSDP_Docs_Inventory_and_Migration_Manifest_V1.0.md` | none installed → governance docs |

If the task spans multiple domains, apply every listed row. When a row has no installed skill, use the listed governance documents and **say so in the compliance report** — never pretend a skill was used.

## 5. Skills Registry (available skills and their locations)

- **Installed skill packages: NONE.** Verified: no `.clinerules/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, or repo `skills/` directories, and no Cline skill configuration in this workspace.
- **Available knowledge artefacts that serve as skill material:** product documents (`docs/product/`), architecture documents (`docs/architecture/`), milestone implementation reports (`docs/implementation/AMSDP_M*`, `docs/implementation/AMSDP_SEQ_*`), the governance assessment and skill-routing architecture (`docs/governance/AMSDP_Engineering_Governance_Assessment.md`, `docs/governance/AMSDP_Skill_Routing_Architecture.md`).
- If an applicable skill is unavailable: report that fact, use the relevant project governance documents, and proceed.
- **Installing any new formal skill requires project-owner approval** (see Skill Routing Architecture §6 for the onboarding path).

## 6. Compliance Reporting (all future milestone reports)

Every implementation report must contain a **Governance and Skill Compliance** section:
- Applicable governance documents (per §4 table) and selected skills.
- Evidence that their instructions were followed.
- Tests and verification performed, with real results.
- Deviations, exceptions, and unresolved issues.
- Skills unavailable or not applicable.

## 7. Evidence Standard

- IMPLEMENTED = code exists and is reachable.
- TESTED = named tests pass in this tree.
- VERIFIED = a second, independent artefact confirms it (browser run, live API call, DB read).
- ACCEPTED = the project owner has signed it.
- A green suite does not make something VERIFIED; documentation does not prove implementation.

## 8. LLM Execution Routing Policy

- **Local Model Priority:** For all simple or isolated tasks (e.g., boilerplate generation, single-file edits, minor functions, styling tweaks), you **must** prioritize using the locally running model (`qwen2.5-coder:1.5b` at `http://localhost:11434`) if it is active.
- **Frontier Escalation:** Only switch to or request a Frontier LLM profile (DeepSeek / OpenRouter) when handling multi-file architecture changes, deep database debugging, or complex algorithmic design.

## 9. Agent Execution Boundaries & Local Hardware Safety

- **Write Code Only:** Focus strictly on writing clean, production-ready code. Do not execute scripts, spin up servers, or run test suites in the terminal unless explicitly requested by the user.
- **Task Triage Limitation:** When working via the local model context, limit edits to isolated tasks (e.g., writing individual functions, updating localized styles, creating unit test objects, or single-file refactors).
- **Yield Protocol:** If a task requires modifying more than 2 files simultaneously, dealing with massive codebase refactor loops, or debugging long terminal stack traces, stop execution immediately and prompt the user to flip the profile to the Frontier LLM.
- **Context Preservation:** Keep logs and terminal reads small. Avoid dump commands that flood the conversation history, as large contexts fill up the remaining 1.8 GB VRAM headroom and slow down execution speeds.
- **No Git Disruptions:** Never run destructive Git adjustments (`git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`, `git stash`, `git checkout .`) to tidy up the tree; roll back with `git revert`. Before staging an edited file, check it is not already carrying someone else's uncommitted work (`git diff <file>`).

